# HTTPS in front of immich-raw on a home network (see README.md).
#
# The certificate is a normal Let's Encrypt one for a DuckDNS name whose address is the server's
# LAN IP. Let's Encrypt checks it with the DNS-01 challenge: Caddy writes a TXT record at DuckDNS
# and Let's Encrypt reads it. With the DNS challenge set, Caddy uses no other challenge, so nothing
# has to be reachable from the internet.
#
# Settings, from the environment:
#   HTTPS_HOST      the DuckDNS name, e.g. my-photos.duckdns.org
#   DUCKDNS_TOKEN   the DuckDNS account token; it only sets and clears the TXT record, never the IP
#   UPSTREAM        where immich-raw is (default immich-raw:80)
#
# immich-raw keeps the X-Forwarded-Proto this sends, so Immich knows the page is HTTPS. Caddy has
# no upload size limit and passes WebSockets as they are, so Immich needs nothing else here.

{$HTTPS_HOST} {
	tls {
		dns duckdns {env.DUCKDNS_TOKEN}
	}
	reverse_proxy {$UPSTREAM:immich-raw:80}
}
